## Slowing CKD down

- **blood pressure medicines**, especially **ACE inhibitors** or **ARBs**, which also protect the kidneys
- **SGLT2 inhibitors**, such as **dapagliflozin**, for many people with CKD, with or without diabetes
- **statins**, to lower the risk of heart attack and stroke
- good control of **diabetes**

Always check with a pharmacist before taking new medicines. **Anti-inflammatory painkillers**, such as ibuprofen, can harm the kidneys.

## Treating complications

You may need treatment for **anaemia**, bone problems, or fluid build-up.

## If the kidneys fail

- **haemodialysis**, where a machine filters your blood, usually 3 times a week in hospital or at home
- **peritoneal dialysis**, where fluid is put into your tummy through a tube to remove waste, usually done at home every day
- a **kidney transplant**, from a living or deceased donor
- **supportive (conservative) care**, which focuses on quality of life without dialysis. Some people choose this, especially older people with other health conditions

Your kidney team will talk to you about the options well before you need them.
